   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-bc",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.9",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Multiple Representations of Limits",
    "stem": "A graph of y = f(x) has an open circle at (2, 5) and a filled point at (2, 8). As x approaches 2 from either side, the graph approaches the open circle. Which limit statement matches this graphical description?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2 f(x) = 8",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Function-value confusion uses the filled point’s height as the limit. The filled point gives f(2) = 8, but nearby graph values approach 5. Fix: Follow the graph from both sides instead of using the filled point."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2 f(x) = 5",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "From both sides of x = 2, the graph’s y-values approach 5. Thus, the graphical description translates to: as x approaches 2, f(x) approaches 5, regardless of the filled point’s height."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2 f(x) = 2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Input-output confusion uses the approaching x-value as the limit of f(x). Here, 2 is the input being approached, while 5 is the output being approached. Fix: Identify the approached x-coordinate and y-coordinate separately before writing the limit."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2 f(x) does not exist",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Open-circle confusion treats a hole as preventing a limit from existing. A limit describes nearby behavior, and the graph approaches the same height from both sides. Fix: Compare the approached heights on both sides before deciding whether the limit exists."
     }
    ]
   },
